<!-- awf:section transitions -->
## Transitions

- `Proposed → Accepted` establishes the first content stamp without applying operations; the body stays amendable via Amended events.
- `Proposed|Accepted → Implementing` appends the status event then a first Applied batch, together with its claim mutations.
- `Implementing → Implementing` appends one or more Applied or Reapplied batches with their matching claim mutations.
- `Implementing → Implemented` appends the terminal status event after implementation has applied every declaration.
- `Proposed|Accepted → Implemented` may remain the direct one-transaction path for all operations; `None.` and one-operation ADRs normally use it.
- `Proposed|Accepted|Implementing → Abandoned` appends the terminal status and rationale. Already Applied operations remain authoritative; Remaining operations become Canceled.

One authored transaction may append several Applied or Reapplied batches only across distinct claim IDs; a repeated same-claim occurrence requires a separately observable authored transaction. It may also append several Status events when the prior history remains an exact prefix and the appended events replay as a legal ordered lifecycle.

`Implemented` and `Abandoned` are terminal. There is no `Superseded` status: an ADR is never active authority, so a later decision changes the affected current-state claims through its own State changes rather than superseding this ADR.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section state-changes -->
## State changes and the claim handshake

An ADR's `## State changes` section is the authoritative link to the topics it governs: either `None.` or a list of `- add`, `- update`, and `- remove` entries, each naming one claim by its qualified `<domain>/<topic>:<slug>` id.

- **At `Accepted`** the operations are settled instruction, amendable under the amendment-until-terminal rules until an Applied event references them. Every operation's destination topic metadata must already exist (an empty topic shell for a pending `add`). The claims describing current reality are unchanged; inspect lifecycle detail where needed with `./awf read adr <identity>`.
- **At `Implementing`** each Applied or Reapplied operation occurrence and exactly its matching claim mutation are independently observable in an authored transaction. Several events and their mutations may share that transaction across distinct claim IDs. An initial `add` appears with this ADR as Origin; an initial `update` preserves Origin and the prior Revised-by prefix, then appends this ADR; a `remove` disappears. A Reapplied add or update makes a further material correction while preserving the provenance written by its first application. Remaining operations continue to appear as pending progress.
- **At `Implemented`** all declarations are Applied. A direct transition uses one implicit batch; an incremental transition has already applied its final batch before the status-only event.
- **At `Abandoned`** Applied operations and their provenance remain historical facts, while Remaining operations become Canceled and authorize nothing.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Procedure

This skill may run without an effort. Pick the status transition first. If continuity independently fired, validate the fixed effort identity and exact `.awf/efforts/<slug>/memory.md`, preserve one user-managed writer, and provide that context read-only to any child or reviewer; otherwise omit effort and memory fields. If changed facts newly make continuity useful, invoke `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` before further mutation. {{if eq .profile "core"}}Project documentation, source, tests, and repository history{{else}}Repository and current-state authority{{end}} remain primary, and standalone memory is forbidden.

<!-- awf:section procedure-status-edit -->
1. **Edit the ADR history and status.** Append the ordered lifecycle events this transaction completes. Account for every explicit Applied batch, including the final batch, whether one transaction carries one or several. A first incremental application includes Implementing status followed by its first Applied event; later application transactions append one or more Applied or Reapplied events. Under the governed awf chain, settled review later appends only Implemented in the status-only terminal transaction after explicit application. Several events may share one transaction only when their operation targets are distinct claim IDs and the prior history remains an exact prefix of a legal ordered lifecycle. Applied grammar is `- YYYY-MM-DD: Applied; operations: <operation-list>`, whose members may appear in any order. If an already-applied add or update needs another material correction, use `- YYYY-MM-DD: Reapplied; operations: <operation-list>`. Reapplied operations must have an earlier Applied occurrence, may repeat across separately observable authored transactions, and do not change progress; removes and events after terminal status are refused. Repeat the latest stamp on status events; an amendment appends `- YYYY-MM-DD: Amended; content-sha256: <digest of the amended content>` while the body remains amendable.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-claim-mutation -->
2. **Apply exactly the transaction's State changes.** Stage the matching add/update/remove edits under `.awf/topics/parts/<domain>/<topic>/current-state.md` in the same commit as their Applied or Reapplied events. Each claim ID appears in at most one operation occurrence in an authored transaction. A Reapplied update preserves its existing canonical Revised-by entry; a Reapplied add preserves Origin naming the ADR and Revised-by byte-for-byte. Never apply Remaining or Canceled operations. On abandonment preserve claims authorized by earlier Applied events.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section state-doc-update -->
3. **Update any current-state topic** whose claims this ADR moves: the claim edits above are that update. Refresh the surrounding explanatory prose in the same topic part when the domain's position has shifted, in the same commit. Do not hand-maintain a per-domain decisions index; the generated domain doc links the topic list instead.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-regen -->
4. **Regenerate INDEX.md.** Run `{{ with .vars.activeMdRegenCmd }}{{ . }}{{ else }}./awf render{{ end }}` to regenerate `{{ .layout.indexMd }}`. Stage the result. Do not hand-edit `INDEX.md`; always regenerate and commit it alongside any ADR status change.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-gate -->
5. **Validate the staged transaction.** Stage the complete transaction; the commit requires `./awf check staged` and {{ with .vars.gateCmd }}`{{ . }}`{{ else }}the project's gate{{ end }} to pass. A wired pre-commit hook enforces both at commit time; run them manually first only in a clone without wired hooks (checkable with `git config core.hooksPath`; when in doubt, run both manually). If either command fails, fix the cause and re-stage before retrying.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section commit-templates -->
## Commit subject templates

Use these subjects for each transition type:

- `docs(adr): accept NNNN <short title>` for `Proposed → Accepted`
- `<feat|fix|refactor>(<scope>): <subject> (applies NNNN batch)` for an explicit application transaction; use a truthful plural subject when it carries several distinct-target batches
- `<feat|fix|refactor>(<scope>): <subject> (implements NNNN)` for direct implicit completion with its matching claim mutations
- `docs(adr): implement NNNN <short title>` for the status-only terminal transaction after explicit application
- `docs(adr): abandon NNNN <short title>` for any legal transition to Abandoned
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section amendment-until-terminal -->
## Amendment-until-terminal

While `status: Proposed`, all sections may be amended freely, with no history event. Once
`Accepted` or `Implementing`, the body stays amendable: each amendment commit appends exactly
one `- YYYY-MM-DD: Amended; content-sha256: <digest of the amended content>` event and changes
nothing else in the history. At `Implemented` or `Abandoned` the body's meaning is frozen; a
schema retrofit may migrate its machine-readable encoding.

- An amendment must not alter or remove a State-changes operation already referenced by an
  Applied event; adding new operations and rewording operations not yet applied stay legal. If
  the declaration is already applied, use Reapplied for an add or update throughout Implementing;
  after terminal status use a follow-up ADR or remove plus add rather than declaring it twice.
- An amendment that changes a Decision item, the State changes operation set, or the meaning
  of an invariant claim is raised as a user-decision before landing, unless it corrects a
  clear defect with a no-brainer fix; prose-only clarification stays autonomous. A
  load-bearing amendment re-dispatches the ADR reviewer over the amended sections before
  landing.
- Commit as `docs(adr): amend NNNN <what changed>`. Deferral (scope shrink) stays a Context
  amendment recorded the same way.
- current-state-v1 records keep the prior rule: frozen once they leave Proposed.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Decision references

V4 Decision items begin with a unique inline `decision: <lowercase-kebab-slug>` marker. Use that stable slug with the ADR number or retained pending ADR slug in plan references. Pre-V4 Decision items have no stable marker: canonical `#N` remains available only for frozen ADR navigation and is not plan-v2 reference syntax; never treat a legacy ordinal as a current-authority or supersession anchor.

<!-- awf:section notes -->
## Notes

- **Append-only rule:** `## Status history` is always append-only; a meaning-preserving schema retrofit may migrate machine-readable encoding.
- Does not commit on your behalf; surfaces the right edits for you to land.
<!-- awf:end -->
